{
  "original_id": "newwin-00001-p18",
  "archive_id": "BW2-N01-P18",
  "title": "Recurrent constant-term words for a degenerate Laurent polynomial",
  "batch_label": "新WIN-1批次",
  "kind": "resolution-paper",
  "original_status": "proved",
  "scope": "For every prime p, set K=F_p and P(x)=x^(p-1)+x^(-1). For every Laurent polynomial Q in K[x,x^(-1)], define u_n=[x^0](P(x)^n Q(x)) for every integer n>=0. Then the following are equivalent: (i) for every i>=0, l>=1, and N>=0 there exists j>N such that u_(j+k)=u_(i+k) for all integers 0<=k<l; (ii) u_n=0 for every n>=0; (iii) Q=P F(P) for some polynomial F in K[t]. The condition in (iii) is the subspace P K[P], not the full Laurent-ring ideal. This covers all primes, including 2, zero Q, and arbitrary finite positive and negative Laurent exponents. It settles source.md for this specified P, without claiming a classification for other degenerate P or higher prime powers.",
